WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.850
Listen well, handle complaints, and say no with respect.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.400
This is the video for module two of Customer Care and Respectful Communication.

3
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:21.110
Most customer problems start with a misunderstanding: the wrong size, the wrong day, pick-up when she meant delivery. Careful listening prevents many complaints.

4
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:30.490
When something does go wrong, a complaint is a chance to fix it. Many unhappy customers never complain. They simply stop coming.

5
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.260
And respectful care does not mean saying yes to everything.

6
00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:46.980
For example, Funmi runs a food business in Ibadan. Mrs Adeyemi calls to order for a church women's meeting. Funmi listens fully, with her notebook open.

7
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:59.430
Open questions invite her to explain. What is the event, and how many people? About 60 women. Is there anything the guests can't eat? About 10 prefer food without pepper.

8
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:12.630
Funmi paraphrases: so, 60 packs, and 10 of them without pepper. Mrs Adeyemi corrects her: 10 of the 60, not 10 extra. Without that, Funmi might have cooked 70 packs.

9
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.290
Then closed questions pin down the date, time, and delivery or pick-up.

10
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:33.860
At about 2,500 naira a pack, that is 150,000 naira, with a 50 percent deposit of 75,000. She reads it all back, then sends the summary by WhatsApp so both have it in writing.

11
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:48.230
For example, Musa runs a dry-cleaning shop in Kaduna. Mr Bello returns angrily. His white agbada, cleaned for about 4,000 naira, has a yellow stain, and he needs it on Saturday.

12
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:58.390
Acknowledge first. Thank you for bringing it back, Sir. I'm sorry, I can see the stain. Avoid non-apologies like sorry if you feel that way.

13
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:07.920
Understand. Musa checks the drop-off ticket, which says no stains. He does not argue, or blame his staff in front of the customer.

14
00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:19.370
Resolve. Option A, a free re-clean by Thursday, with a full refund if the stain remains. Option B, an immediate refund. Mr Bello chooses A.

15
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:30.920
Musa writes the promise on a new ticket, calls when it is ready, and later asks how the wedding went. Mr Bello comes back the next month with three more outfits.

16
00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:39.640
A respectful no has three parts. No, said clearly. A short, honest reason. And what you can do instead.

17
00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:53.050
Mrs Okon picks goods worth 12,000 naira and asks to pay at month end. Grace says: Aunty, I'm sorry, I can't give credit anymore. When some debts weren't paid, I couldn't restock.

18
00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:01.570
Then the alternative. They choose the most urgent items, rice and cooking oil, for the 5,000 she has today.

19
00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.220
Grace holds the other 7,000 naira of goods, with her name on them, for three days.

20
00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:18.470
Mrs Okon is a little disappointed but not insulted, and keeps shopping with Grace. Say the reason once, kindly, and keep your rules the same for everyone.

21
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:27.290
People tend to match the volume around them, so lower your voice and slow down. Let them finish. Interrupting adds fuel.

22
00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:33.020
Use their title and name the feeling: Sir, I can hear you're very frustrated.

23
00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:39.840
Move to a quieter spot if you can, and offer a choice, which gives back a sense of control.

24
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:54.190
Frustration is understandable. Abuse is not. Give one calm warning: I can't continue while you insult me. If it continues, end the conversation and ask a colleague or neighbour to stand with you.

25
00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:04.910
If a customer threatens you or becomes physical, move away, call for help, and let the goods or money go. You can report serious threats afterwards.

26
00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:17.020
If someone says food tastes or smells off, apologise, replace or refund it, and remove that batch until you have checked it. Food safety comes before any argument.

27
00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:22.180
On WhatsApp, you can stop replying, and block and report the number.

28
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:33.490
To remember. Ask open questions first. Paraphrase and confirm every detail. Handle complaints with feelings first, facts second, then two options.

29
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.110
Say no with a reason and an alternative. And put your safety first.

30
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:47.530
Now take one fully confirmed order, rewrite a defensive reply in four parts, and practise three respectful no's aloud.

31
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.820
Continue with the lessons below.
